All right.

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:34.620
So Indranil, you're
the PM, so I'm going

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:36.180
to ask you the hard question.

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:39.510
So 30,000 foot view,
what is IoT Core

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:41.175
from a really basic standpoint?

00:06:41.175 --> 00:06:42.300
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY: Sure.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:46.320
So if you look at
Google Cloud Platform,

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:50.320
we have what we believe
is a state of the art,

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:54.450
well-received data analytics
and machine learning services

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:56.580
today, available
for our enterprise

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:59.430
customers, our developers,
and our partners as well.

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:02.280
And what Cloud IoT
Core does is, it really

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:07.140
makes it easy to onboard
your IoT devices, things that

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:10.350
are in your factory, in
your oil and gas rigs

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:12.930
or other enterprise
setting to easily

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.290
onboard those devices
to Google Cloud

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.550
so that you can start pushing
data from your devices

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:19.530
to Google Cloud,
and start benefiting

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.840
from our data analytics and
machine learning services.

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:25.290
So that's essentially
what Cloud IoT Core does.

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:28.950
It is a fully-managed service
to securely connect and manage

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:31.784
your IoT devices on
Google Cloud platform.

00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:32.700
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:37.110
So when you say to securely
connect, what I'm expecting

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:42.929
is there's some secure way to
even start the communication.

00:07:42.929 --> 00:07:45.345
How do you make sure that those
devices are those devices?

00:07:45.345 --> 00:07:49.605
Do you expect the unit to
install certificates in them?

00:07:49.605 --> 00:07:52.664
What do you mean by
securely communicate?

00:07:52.664 --> 00:07:54.330
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY:
Yeah, so we deeply

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:56.685
care about security
for the devices

00:07:56.685 --> 00:07:58.560
as well as the data that
is being transmitted

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:00.120
from the device to Cloud.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:02.270
So first, as you
mentioned Francesc,

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:04.620
it's really about
secure connection

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:06.030
of the device to the Cloud.

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:11.250
So IoT Core supports asymetric
key based authentication

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:13.030
in each individual device.

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:15.870
So what that means is, you
will have a private key which

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:19.170
you store securely-- which
it will store in the device,

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:21.390
and then you would
register a public key

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:25.080
from the device based on the
private key onto Cloud IoT Core

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:28.680
so when the device makes a
connection to Cloud IoT Core,

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.740
we can verify based on the
signature of the device

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.450
that it sends with
the public key

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:36.900
to make sure that that device
is really what it says it is.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:41.850
And we use standard JWT
token for authentication

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:44.557
between the device and IoT Core.

00:08:44.557 --> 00:08:46.140
So the device will
use the private key

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:48.790
to sign a JWT token, and then
it will send the JWT token

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:52.560
to IoT Core, and then we use
the JWT token to then verify

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:55.060
the signature with our public
key that has been registered.

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:57.870
So that's one aspect, where
we authenticate each device

00:08:57.870 --> 00:08:59.320
at an individual level.

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:02.040
And second is, even
for a connection

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.180
we support TLS 1.2 and above.

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:07.240
So we expect that each
device will use TLS 1.2

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:09.660
to make the initial
connection to Google Cloud IoT

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:10.830
Core over TCP.

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:14.310
Anything outside, if it's not
TLS, we reject that connection.

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:15.390
That's the first step.

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:19.180
And then further as that data
gets transmitted to Cloud IoT

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:21.910
Core, whether it's
at rest in the Cloud

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:23.580
or whether it's in
transit, the data

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:24.870
is always in encrypted form.

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:29.710
So that's the way we ensure
security at the device level

00:09:29.710 --> 00:09:32.430
during connection as well as
at the data level as well.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:35.340
MARK MANDEL: And also
for the TLS connection

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:37.770
on the other end,
the device will also

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:39.930
need to have a root
certificate on it

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:42.750
to verify on the other side that
the device knows it's actually

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:43.800
talking to Google.

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:46.320
So you can either use
Google provided root cert,

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.390
or when you create your bucket
in GCP for your devices--

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:51.390
we call it the registry.

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:52.806
When you create
your registry, you

00:09:52.806 --> 00:09:54.556
can specify a third
party root certificate

00:09:54.556 --> 00:09:55.972
if you want to use
something else.

00:09:55.972 --> 00:09:57.557
So that's the
other on the device

00:09:57.557 --> 00:09:59.640
side to make sure it knows
it's talking to Google.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:00.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:02.066
I have a bunch of
questions, but the first one

00:10:02.066 --> 00:10:04.920
is, you said JWT
token many times.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:05.870
Is that J-W-T?

00:10:08.227 --> 00:10:09.310
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY: Yes.

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:09.847
It is JWT.

00:10:09.847 --> 00:10:11.180
It's essentially JSON Web Token.

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:12.263
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK, cool.

00:10:12.263 --> 00:10:13.275
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:10:13.275 --> 00:10:16.730
All right, so you covered
the security side of it.

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:19.420
What's the communication
part of it?

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:20.749
What does that actually mean?

00:10:20.749 --> 00:10:23.040
Are they just sending faxes
to each other or something?

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:24.010
Or what's the deal?

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:25.051
GABE WEISS: Smoke signal.

00:10:25.051 --> 00:10:26.904
It's all smoke signal.

00:10:26.904 --> 00:10:30.470
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY: [LAUGHS]
So as part of Cloud IoT Core,

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:34.120
we realized that MQTT
is a protocol which

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:37.730
is pretty standard
in the industry.

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:41.920
And so we started with a
native support for MQTT

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:43.820
on Cloud IoT Core.

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:49.030
So now with MQTT, devices
can publish telemetry, even

00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:52.990
other information on
MQTT topic to IoT Core.

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:55.690
And IoT Core then
aggregates all the data

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:57.700
across all the devices,
and then pushes

00:10:57.700 --> 00:11:00.160
it downstream to
Pub/Sub topic from where

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:01.750
you can consume,
and then send it

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:05.185
downstream to other services
such as BigQuery or BigTable

00:11:05.185 --> 00:11:07.000
and as such.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:10.390
So really, the
transmission between device

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:14.362
and communication between
device and IoT Core

00:11:14.362 --> 00:11:15.570
will depend on the protocols.

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:19.480
If you use MQTT, then you
can publish your telemetry

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:21.910
on a certain topic and
then we take it from there.

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:24.340
If you're using HTTP, we
also have support for HTTP,

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:25.060
as you know.

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:28.420
If you use HTTP, then you would
do a request-response protocol

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:31.360
to send your data and
telemetry to Cloud IoT Core.

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:34.000
MARK MANDEL: And MQTT, is
that a bidirectional protocol,

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:36.850
or is it just sending data
from the device down to Cloud?

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:38.891
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY: I'm
glad you brought it up.

00:11:38.891 --> 00:11:40.690
Yes, MQTT is a
bidirectional protocol.

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:42.190
It is based on TCP.

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:44.680
And the way it works is,
your devices will publish

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:47.170
certain data to a
particular topic,

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:50.530
and Cloud can also published
data to a certain topic

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:53.860
for the device to subscribe
to and get the data.

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:55.030
So it is bidirectional.

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:57.580
And IoT Core supports
bidirectional data

00:11:57.580 --> 00:11:59.372
between the device and
the Cloud over MQTT.

00:11:59.372 --> 00:12:01.246
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Now
that we're in the center

00:12:01.246 --> 00:12:03.160
of the debate what
this product does

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:07.060
and the security
part of it, I am

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:10.290
curious about what is the
audience of this product?

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:15.550
Meaning if I'm building, I
don't know, smart lightbulbs,

00:12:15.550 --> 00:12:16.420
is this for me?

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:18.670
Or if it's I'm
building something

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:21.400
like a home hack where
I have something like I

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:22.870
have just one little device--

00:12:22.870 --> 00:12:24.869
I'm actually trying to
work on this, by the way.

00:12:24.869 --> 00:12:25.510
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:30.410
Something that allows me to open
the garage door from my phone.

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:32.410
What is-- who is this for?

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:34.990
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY: Yeah,
so Cloud IoT Core is really

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:36.760
built for scale, right?

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:40.870
So if you think about
an enterprise company

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:44.350
or a smart parking company,
they have thousands and hundreds

00:12:44.350 --> 00:12:46.960
of thousands of devices
installed in different parking

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:49.180
lots and on the curbside.

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:51.220
And all of these
devices and sensors

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:53.530
are sending data
pretty frequently.

00:12:53.530 --> 00:12:56.280
So they've been able to connect
all those devices to Cloud IoT

00:12:56.280 --> 00:13:00.200
Core and ingest
the data at-scale.

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:04.050
So IoT Core is really
designed to connect

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:07.530
massive amount of devices and
just a massive scale of data

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:10.270
without a customer
or the partner, even

00:13:10.270 --> 00:13:13.432
the developer needing to
worry about the infrastructure

00:13:13.432 --> 00:13:14.140
and provisioning.

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:15.482
You just connect your devices.

00:13:15.482 --> 00:13:17.190
Whether you're connecting
one or millions

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:19.960
of devices or even billions,
we will scale internally.

00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:22.420
We will be able to ingest data
at-scale without an issue.

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:24.670
So that's what is really
designed and built for.

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:26.290
Now having said
that, we understand

00:13:26.290 --> 00:13:29.560
that in a lot of
developers enterprise,

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:32.350
and even the
commercial developers,

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:34.180
they wanted to get
their hands dirty,

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:36.560
they want to build
some quick prototype

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:38.720
before they deploy at-scale.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:44.050
So we made it easy and simple
enough for even the developers

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:47.470
who wants to jumpstart
their journey on IoT to be

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:51.190
able to quickly build some
prototype using IoT Core.

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:53.990
So we do have a
rich set of APIs.

00:13:53.990 --> 00:13:56.920
At the same time, we have a
pretty simple user interface

00:13:56.920 --> 00:13:59.740
for any developer, or even
an operator for that matter,

00:13:59.740 --> 00:14:01.840
to quickly build
some quick prototype

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:04.820
to test out the product
before they scale.

00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:07.875
So really, going back
to your question,

00:14:07.875 --> 00:14:09.265
it is designed for scale.

00:14:09.265 --> 00:14:14.650
But at the same time, we expect
a wide spectrum of customers

00:14:14.650 --> 00:14:16.120
and developers to use it.

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:18.620
Developers who are building
it for a massive scale

00:14:18.620 --> 00:14:20.290
and for an enterprise
grade product.

00:14:20.290 --> 00:14:23.140
At the same time, developers who
want to get their hands dirty,

00:14:23.140 --> 00:14:24.867
they want to build
some quick prototype

00:14:24.867 --> 00:14:26.450
before they graduate
to the next level

00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:28.810
where they deploy
full scale devices.

00:14:28.810 --> 00:14:29.530
GABE WEISS: Nice.

00:14:29.530 --> 00:14:30.190
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:14:30.190 --> 00:14:31.240
So, Gabe, I'm going
to direct this

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:32.410
to you because I know
you've been building stuff

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:33.490
with IoT Core.

00:14:33.490 --> 00:14:35.449
What does the development
experience look like?

00:14:35.449 --> 00:14:36.990
GABE WEISS: I'm
pretty new to Google.

00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:38.260
I just started in May.

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:40.600
So one of the hardest
ramp ups I've had

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:43.210
is learning all the different
pieces of GCP, learning all

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:45.550
the APIs, how to build stuff.

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:48.430
IoT Core is by far the easiest
I've worked with so far,

00:14:48.430 --> 00:14:49.422
hands down.

00:14:49.422 --> 00:14:50.380
It's a managed service.

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:51.610
So you don't have to
stand anything up,

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:54.490
you don't have to start a GCE,
you don't have to do anything.

00:14:54.490 --> 00:14:55.270
It's an end point.

00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:57.437
So what that means is, I'm
free to sit on my device,

00:14:57.437 --> 00:14:59.936
and I implement the three pieces
that I need to worry about.

00:14:59.936 --> 00:15:01.812
I need to worry about
the JWT authentication.

00:15:01.812 --> 00:15:03.977
I need to worry about
initializing the communication

00:15:03.977 --> 00:15:04.510
piece.

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:06.430
And then I need my sensor
data, my telemetry,

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:08.067
whatever that ends up being.

00:15:08.067 --> 00:15:08.650
And that's it.

00:15:08.650 --> 00:15:12.010
I don't have to download
any SDKs from Google Cloud,

00:15:12.010 --> 00:15:14.740
I don't have to implement
any funky Cloud stuff.

00:15:14.740 --> 00:15:19.060
I can just put this message
together in MQTT, publish it,

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:21.070
and Google handles
the rest of it.

00:15:21.070 --> 00:15:23.740
And I end up with data in
my Cloud Pub/Sub topic.

00:15:23.740 --> 00:15:26.260
So from a development
standpoint,

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:28.000
I have to pick my
library of choice.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:29.630
I've worked with
Python, Note.js,

00:15:29.630 --> 00:15:33.190
there's some C libraries that
work with just bare metal.

00:15:33.190 --> 00:15:35.170
I just did this
last week when I was

00:15:35.170 --> 00:15:37.150
at the conference in Barcelona.

00:15:37.150 --> 00:15:40.100
It was a 10 minute theater
talk where it was bare

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:41.300
device, nothing on it.

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:45.135
And at the end of 10 minutes,
you have your messages in Cloud

00:15:45.135 --> 00:15:46.260
that you can do stuff with.

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:47.470
So it's super easy.

00:15:47.470 --> 00:15:48.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And you didn't have

00:15:48.340 --> 00:15:50.290
to install anything
specific for IoT Core?

00:15:50.290 --> 00:15:53.020
I'm assuming you're just putting
in some sort of MQTT client

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:54.550
on the device that's very
standard that you don't

00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:55.080
have to worry about otherwise?

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:56.230
GABE WEISS: Yeah, so
there's standard--

00:15:56.230 --> 00:15:58.060
that's the three
languages I mentioned.

00:15:58.060 --> 00:16:00.420
So they're just standard
libraries for each one.

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:03.910
Paho MQTT is the
kind of de facto MQTT

00:16:03.910 --> 00:16:06.080
library across all of them.

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:08.540
JWT, there's a PyJWT for Python.

00:16:08.540 --> 00:16:09.655
It's one that I was using.

00:16:09.655 --> 00:16:12.280
So those are the only things you
need to install on the device.

00:16:12.280 --> 00:16:14.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And I suppose
then also that means then

00:16:14.050 --> 00:16:16.210
that you're not tied to
a particular hardware

00:16:16.210 --> 00:16:18.515
provider or a particular
set of IoT device,

00:16:18.515 --> 00:16:20.140
you can basically
run this on anything.

00:16:20.140 --> 00:16:20.950
GABE WEISS: On
pretty much anything

00:16:20.950 --> 00:16:22.199
that can talk to the internet.

00:16:22.199 --> 00:16:23.920
So we've done everything
from the lowest

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:25.330
level, the microcontrollers.

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:27.880
The ESP8266 are these little
nice Wi-Fi controllers

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:32.140
that are super slim, don't
do anything other than Wi-Fi.

00:16:32.140 --> 00:16:35.200
And we've got it to work on
those through Arduino, Pi,

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:36.677
and then on some
of the industrial.

00:16:36.677 --> 00:16:39.010
We've worked with a couple
of partners and their devices

00:16:39.010 --> 00:16:40.510
to get those talking
to Core also.

00:16:40.510 --> 00:16:41.426
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:16:41.426 --> 00:16:44.200
So I'm curious about the
developing experience.

00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:47.200
You're saying that you have
basically a Pub/Sub queue

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:49.520
and that you're receiving
all the messages from there,

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:52.120
which seems relatively easy.

00:16:52.120 --> 00:16:54.700
I'm imagining that when you
receive that, in the payload

00:16:54.700 --> 00:16:56.500
there's some
identification of what

00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:58.750
is the origin, what
is the device exactly

00:16:58.750 --> 00:17:00.550
that is sending the data?

00:17:00.550 --> 00:17:02.680
GABE WEISS: You
don't necessarily

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:03.991
unless you put it in there.

00:17:03.991 --> 00:17:04.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, OK.

00:17:04.990 --> 00:17:07.329
GABE WEISS: You have
a record of the device

00:17:07.329 --> 00:17:09.030
as it's registered
in the system,

00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:11.488
and there's some ID in there,
but there wouldn't be any way

00:17:11.488 --> 00:17:14.710
to tie a Pub/Sub message
back to your device

00:17:14.710 --> 00:17:16.669
necessarily unless you
added it to the payload.

00:17:16.669 --> 00:17:18.585
Which is best practice,
we recommend of course

00:17:18.585 --> 00:17:19.420
that you do that.

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:24.069
But the bridge in IoT
Core, it's a pass-through.

00:17:24.069 --> 00:17:25.900
So as the messages
come into IoT Core,

00:17:25.900 --> 00:17:27.934
it bundles it up
with some metadata.

00:17:27.934 --> 00:17:29.350
Indranil, correct
me if I'm wrong,

00:17:29.350 --> 00:17:31.516
but I don't think it actually
identifies the device.

00:17:31.516 --> 00:17:33.150
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY:
We do have--

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:35.640
so Pub/Sup has this notion
of a message attributes,

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:37.410
I think as you guys know.

00:17:37.410 --> 00:17:40.270
So as part of
message attribute, we

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:43.820
do put the device ID as part
of the message attribute.

00:17:43.820 --> 00:17:46.510
So when you're
consuming the data

00:17:46.510 --> 00:17:48.345
as a subscriber from
Pub/Sub, every message

00:17:48.345 --> 00:17:49.720
you get you can
read the message.

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:52.300
After which then you can
use that to know that

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:53.920
this particular
message has been sent

00:17:53.920 --> 00:17:56.150
by the thermostat
in your living room

00:17:56.150 --> 00:17:59.390
versus the thermostat in
your bedroom, for example.

00:17:59.390 --> 00:18:01.030
So the Pub/Sub
message attribute,

00:18:01.030 --> 00:18:03.490
which is where we
add the device ID.

00:18:03.490 --> 00:18:04.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:18:04.720 --> 00:18:08.180
So once you have that ID,
how do you reply to it?

00:18:08.180 --> 00:18:11.067
What if you want to send a
message to a specific device?

00:18:11.067 --> 00:18:12.900
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY:
So there's two things.

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:15.370
So one is, for
every time a device

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:17.434
publishes a message,
a telemetry,

00:18:17.434 --> 00:18:18.850
could be a temperature
measurement

00:18:18.850 --> 00:18:21.880
or could be a pressure
measurement to Cloud IoT Core,

00:18:21.880 --> 00:18:23.440
if IoT Core then could bridge.

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:26.100
If it's able to publish
that message successfully

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:29.070
to Pub/Sub, it will send
an ACK back to the device.

00:18:29.070 --> 00:18:31.930
So the device that we know that
the message that it had sent,

00:18:31.930 --> 00:18:34.270
the measurement, that data
that it has sent to IoT Core

00:18:34.270 --> 00:18:36.130
has been received
and acknowledged.

00:18:36.130 --> 00:18:37.550
That's one way of communication.

00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:41.080
Then the next is,
as a developer you

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:44.170
may want to send some message
back to the device, right,

00:18:44.170 --> 00:18:47.142
or some communication,
some control command

00:18:47.142 --> 00:18:48.850
back to the device,
or some configuration

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:50.270
update back to the device.

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:52.000
So we have-- part
of IoT Core, there

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:55.750
are APIs which will allow you
to directly send a message

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:57.890
to a specific device ID.

00:18:57.890 --> 00:19:00.160
So you can say, I want
to change the temperature

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:02.180
setting of the thermostat
in the living room.

00:19:02.180 --> 00:19:04.138
You can specify the device
ID of the thermostat

00:19:04.138 --> 00:19:06.910
and send a specific
configuration change

00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:07.786
to the device.

00:19:07.786 --> 00:19:09.160
And the good thing
about IoT Core

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:15.230
is, with IoT, the connectivity
is sort of intermittent.

00:19:15.230 --> 00:19:17.502
Most of the time it's
there, but sometimes

00:19:17.502 --> 00:19:19.960
because of bandwidth constraint
or because of your location

00:19:19.960 --> 00:19:23.290
or whatnot, it's not always
continuously connected.

00:19:23.290 --> 00:19:25.510
So IoT Core makes sure that
when you send the latest

00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:28.352
configuration, when the
device-- if the device

00:19:28.352 --> 00:19:30.310
is not connected, when
the device comes back up

00:19:30.310 --> 00:19:32.590
and connects, it will send
the latest configuration

00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:34.130
to the device.

00:19:34.130 --> 00:19:35.695
So from an application
perspective,

00:19:35.695 --> 00:19:39.910
you build your
application and you're not

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:43.210
worried about the upstream
connection and the data flow

00:19:43.210 --> 00:19:45.010
between the Cloud and device.

00:19:45.010 --> 00:19:46.150
IoT Core takes care of it.

00:19:46.150 --> 00:19:47.066
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:19:47.066 --> 00:19:49.276
MARK MANDEL: So serverless
is the cool new buzzword.

00:19:49.276 --> 00:19:50.644
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:50.644 --> 00:19:53.410
So it's going to be serverless
or ML, one or other.

00:19:53.410 --> 00:19:56.770
I'm looking at diagrams here
on the Cloud IoT Core website,

00:19:56.770 --> 00:20:00.220
and I see that there's a way
to hook in Cloud Functions.

00:20:00.220 --> 00:20:02.780
Or is Cloud Functions
just really useful here?

00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:03.900
Where does that fit in?

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:05.350
GABE WEISS: Cloud Functions
is just super useful.

00:20:05.350 --> 00:20:06.760
Because with Cloud
Functions, you

00:20:06.760 --> 00:20:09.919
have the hook for Pub/Sub
message getting subscribed.

00:20:09.919 --> 00:20:11.960
So since IoT Core connects
directly into Pub/Sub,

00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:14.790
so everything you publish to
IoT Core goes into Pub/Sub,

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:16.860
your Cloud Functions
now can hook into that

00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:20.400
and do stuff based on
whatever you want it to.

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:23.070
So you can route
things to-- you can

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:25.900
use Cloud Functions to route the
data from Pub/Sub to anywhere

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:26.400
you want.

00:20:26.400 --> 00:20:30.280
Store it in a database, send
it along to BigQuery or machine

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:30.780
learning.

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:33.882
All of the stuff that
might be overkill for some

00:20:33.882 --> 00:20:35.590
of the other systems,
GCP, Cloud Function

00:20:35.590 --> 00:20:36.840
is just a super
nice, lightweight way

00:20:36.840 --> 00:20:37.800
to do some of that stuff.

00:20:37.800 --> 00:20:38.260
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY: Yeah.

00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:40.620
In fact, if you think
about a simple use case

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:43.440
where you're reading
the temperature setting

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:47.082
from your thermostat, or then
you're consuming it to Pub/Sub,

00:20:47.082 --> 00:20:48.540
you can have a
Cloud Function which

00:20:48.540 --> 00:20:50.550
reads the temperature setting.

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:52.260
And when it goes
above 80 degrees,

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:56.400
it can send a message back to
the same thermostat to say,

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:58.310
turn off the air condition.

00:20:58.310 --> 00:21:00.330
So there's a Cloud
Function in conjunction

00:21:00.330 --> 00:21:03.030
with, like Gabe mentioned
with Pub/Sub topic,

00:21:03.030 --> 00:21:05.430
can really be used to build
a very simple business

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:07.380
logic with IoT Core.

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:11.700
So I think that's one area
where we do see developers

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:14.340
trying out and building simple
rules and business logics.

00:21:14.340 --> 00:21:15.256
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:21:15.256 --> 00:21:17.970
I just thought about
if this then that.

00:21:17.970 --> 00:21:19.470
That's basically
what you're saying.

00:21:19.470 --> 00:21:22.080
You're able to build a
system that is basically

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:27.240
just events and Cloud Functions
as the glue connecting them.

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:29.240
GABE WEISS: Yeah, and
stay completely serverless

00:21:29.240 --> 00:21:29.930
in that fashion.

00:21:29.930 --> 00:21:32.070
So you don't have to have
any of these functions living

00:21:32.070 --> 00:21:33.750
somewhere, they just
live in the Cloud.

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:34.958
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's cool.

